{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-223",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "Longitudinal rod AB at rest in K1, moving along the shared X/X1 direction relative to K; transverse axes remain perpendicular. Two explicitly added synchronized K clocks show simultaneous endpoint measurement, not visual appearance.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-223.png",
      "sha256": "871c361eaba6893863778eafe815ef5e0c2ff5274b26ca9be2c51dc876d80170"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "参考系 $K$ 与棒有相对运动，相对速度为 $v$。\n硬质棒在惯性参考系 $K_1$ 中静止，如图 223 所示。\n在 $K_1$ 中测得的棒长称为静长，也叫固有长度，记为 $l_0$。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "在参考系 $K$ 中测量运动棒时，必须按这个参考系的同步钟同时记录棒的两端位置。\n两端位置之间的距离就是这个参考系测得的棒长 $l$。\n它与静长的关系为："
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "$l=l_0\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$ (8.2)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "当相对速度非零且小于光速时，收缩因子满足：\n$\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}<1$"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "因此，沿相对运动方向测得的运动棒长度小于同一根棒的静长。\n垂直于相对运动方向的尺寸不发生这种长度收缩。\n相对速度为零时，测得的长度等于静长。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "这种距离的相对性，指的是同一根棒两端间的测量距离随参考系而变。\n它与各惯性参考系中光速相同、时间间隔具有相对性这两条规律相联系。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "仍用上一节的光学钟来推导长度关系。\n这次把棒放在与两参考系相对运动速度平行的方向，而不是上一节的垂直方向。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "棒的一端 $A$ 发出光脉冲，另一端 $B$ 的镜子把它反射回来，如图 224 所示。\n在参考系 $K_1$ 中，脉冲离开 A 后又回到 A，棒上的钟测得往返时间 $\\tau_0$。\n棒的静长为 $l_0$，去程和回程各走一个静长，因此时间 $\\tau_0$ 满足：\n$c\\tau_0=2l_0$ (8.3)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "在参考系 $K$ 中，起始事件是 $A$ 发出信号，结束事件是信号在镜 $B$ 反射后回到移动的 A。\n这个参考系用自己的同步钟记录两事件的时刻，时间间隔记为 $\\tau$。\n棒上钟测得的固有时间间隔 $\\tau_0$ 与这里的 $\\tau$ 满足上一节的公式(8.1)。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "把光脉冲从发射端到镜子的去程时间记为 $\\tau_1$，即从 $A$ 到 $B$ 的传播时间。\n把反方向的回程时间记为 $\\tau_2$，这些时间都按参考系 K 的钟计量。\n往返总时间为：\n$\\tau=\\tau_1+\\tau_2$。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "图 225 给出棒在参考系 $K$ 中的三个位置。\n发射时棒位于 $AB$，经过 $\\tau_1$ 到达反射时的位置 $A_1B_1$，经过 $\\tau_1+\\tau_2$ 到达接收时的位置 $A_2B_2$。\n在去程时间 $\\tau_1$ 内，棒相对于 $K$ 前进了 $v\\tau_1$。\n\n光从 $A$ 向 $B$ 传播时，镜子不断远离发射位置。\n因此在 $K$ 中，去程路程为 $l+v\\tau_1$，其中 $l$ 是这个参考系同时测得的棒长。\n光速仍为 c，所以：\n$l+v\\tau_1=c\\tau_1$。\n\n解得：\n$\\tau_1=\\frac{l}{c-v}$。\n光脉冲从 $B$ 返回 $A$ 时，在参考系 $K$ 中的回程路程为 $l-v\\tau_2$。\n\n因为在时间 $\\tau_2$ 内，接收端 $A$ 迎着光脉冲前进了 $v\\tau_2$。\n于是：\n$l-v\\tau_2=c\\tau_2$。\n\n解得：\n$\\tau_2=\\frac{l}{c+v}$。\n参考系 $K$ 的钟记录的往返总时间为：\n$\\tau=\\tau_1+\\tau_2=\\frac{2cl}{c^2-v^2}=\\frac{2l}{c}\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$ (8.4)\n\n再用时间间隔关系(8.1)和静长关系(8.3)，同一个总时间也可写为：\n$\\tau=\\frac{\\tau_0}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}}=\\frac{2l_0}{c}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}}$ (8.5)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "公式(8.4)和(8.5)表示同一对事件之间的时间间隔，因此可以令它们的右边相等。\n约去共同因子，便得到静长与运动长度之间的关系(8.2)。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "**沿运动方向测得的长度不是绝对量，它取决于物体相对于测量参考系的速度**。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "对同一根棒作比较，静长是它最大的纵向测量长度。\n如果改为让棒在参考系 $K$ 中静止，则它在这里的长度为 $l_0$，而在参考系 $K_1$ 中测得的纵向长度按公式(8.2)缩短。\n这符合相对性原理，并不意味着存在优先的惯性参考系。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "因此，运动长度的缩短具有相互性。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "原书引用爱丁格顿，提醒读者这种相互收缩会挑战日常直觉。\n可以比较两根静长相同、分别在两个惯性参考系中静止并沿相对运动方向放置的棒。\n每个参考系中的观察者都测得另一根运动棒比本地静止棒短。\n\n这里比较的是按各自参考系的钟同时记录的两端位置，而不是看到的外观。\n两个参考系对同时性的判定不同，因此这些对称的测量结论并不矛盾。"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Frame $K$ moves relative to the rod at speed $v$.\nThe rigid rod is at rest in inertial frame $K_1$, as shown in Figure 223.\nIts length measured in $K_1$ is its rest length, or proper length, denoted by $l_0$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "To measure the moving rod in frame $K$, record both endpoint positions simultaneously according to that frame's synchronized clocks.\nTheir separation is the length $l$ measured in that frame.\nIts relation to the proper length is:"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "$l=l_0\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$ (8.2)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "For a nonzero relative speed below the speed of light, the contraction factor satisfies:\n$\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}<1$"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "The moving length along the direction of relative motion is therefore smaller than the same rod's proper length.\nDimensions perpendicular to the relative motion do not undergo this length contraction.\nAt zero relative speed, the measured length equals the proper length."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Here, relativity of distance means that the measured separation of the same rod's endpoints depends on the reference frame.\nIt is connected to the same light speed in all inertial frames and to the relativity of time intervals."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Use the light clock from the preceding section to derive the length relation.\nThis time the rod is parallel to the relative velocity of the two frames, rather than perpendicular as before."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "A light pulse leaves endpoint $A$ and is reflected by the mirror at endpoint $B$, as shown in Figure 224.\nIn frame $K_1$, the pulse returns to A, and the clock on the rod measures the round-trip time $\\tau_0$.\n\nThe rod's proper length is $l_0$, and each leg covers one proper length, so the time $\\tau_0$ satisfies:\n$c\\tau_0=2l_0$ (8.3)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "In frame $K$, the first event is emission at $A$, and the second is reception at the moving A after reflection at mirror $B$.\nThis frame uses its own synchronized clocks to assign times to the events.\n\nTheir interval is $\\tau$.\nThe proper time $\\tau_0$ on the rod's clock and the interval $\\tau$ are related by equation (8.1) from the preceding section."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Let $\\tau_1$ be the outbound travel time from $A$ to $B$.\nLet $\\tau_2$ be the return travel time in the opposite direction, with both times measured in frame K.\n\nThe total round-trip time is:\n$\\tau=\\tau_1+\\tau_2$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Figure 225 shows three positions of the rod in frame $K$.\nAt emission the rod is at $AB$.\nAfter time $\\tau_1$ it is at $A_1B_1$ for reflection.\n\nAfter time $\\tau_1+\\tau_2$ it is at $A_2B_2$ for reception.\nDuring the outbound time $\\tau_1$, the rod moves relative to $K$ through a distance $v\\tau_1$.\n\nAs the pulse travels from $A$ toward $B$, the mirror moves away from the emission position.\nThe outbound path in $K$ is therefore $l+v\\tau_1$, where $l$ is the rod length measured simultaneously in this frame.\n\nLight still travels at speed c, so:\n$l+v\\tau_1=c\\tau_1$.\nSolving gives:\n$\\tau_1=\\frac{l}{c-v}$.\n\nWhen the pulse returns from $B$ to $A$, its path in frame $K$ is $l-v\\tau_2$.\nDuring time $\\tau_2$, the receiving endpoint $A$ moves toward the pulse through a distance $v\\tau_2$.\n\nThus:\n$l-v\\tau_2=c\\tau_2$.\nSolving gives:\n$\\tau_2=\\frac{l}{c+v}$.\n\nThe round-trip time recorded by the clocks of $K$ is:\n$\\tau=\\tau_1+\\tau_2=\\frac{2cl}{c^2-v^2}=\\frac{2l}{c}\\frac{1}{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}$ (8.4)\n\nUsing the time-interval relation (8.1) and the rest-length relation (8.3), the same total time is also:\n$\\tau=\\frac{\\tau_0}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}}=\\frac{2l_0}{c}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-\\frac{v^2}{c^2}}}$ (8.5)"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "Equations (8.4) and (8.5) describe the interval between the same two events, so their right-hand sides are equal.\nCanceling the common factors gives the relation (8.2) between the proper length and the moving length."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "**Length measured along the direction of motion is not absolute, because it depends on the object's speed relative to the measuring frame**."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "For the same rod, the proper length is its greatest measured longitudinal length.\nIf instead the rod is at rest in frame $K$, its length there is $l_0$, while its longitudinal length measured in frame $K_1$ is contracted according to equation (8.2).\nThis agrees with the relativity principle and does not select a preferred inertial frame."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "The contraction of moving lengths is therefore reciprocal."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s4-n97",
        "text": "The source cites Eddington to note that reciprocal contraction challenges everyday intuition.\nCompare two rods of equal proper length, each at rest in a different inertial frame and aligned with their relative motion.\n\nObservers in each frame measure the other, moving rod to be shorter than their local, stationary rod.\nThey compare endpoint positions recorded simultaneously according to their own frame's clocks, not visual appearances.\nThe frames use different simultaneity conditions, so these symmetric measurements do not contradict each other."
      }
    ],
